# Banco de lecciones — Learn British English
# Estructura: { "nivel": [ {"titulo": ..., "parrafo": ...}, ... ] }

LECCIONES = {
    "A1 – Principiante": [
        {"titulo": "My Family",
         "parrafo": ("My name is Tom. I live in a small flat in London with my family. My mother is a nurse and my father is a bus driver. I have one sister. Her name is Emma. She is seven years old. We have a cat called Whiskers. Every morning, I eat toast and drink tea for breakfast.")},
        {"titulo": "A Day at School",
         "parrafo": ("I go to school by bus. My school is near the park. Lessons start at nine o'clock. My favourite subject is history. At lunchtime, I eat sandwiches with my friends. After school, I play football in the garden. I do my homework before dinner.")},
        {"titulo": "My House",
         "parrafo": ("My house has two floors. On the ground floor, there is a kitchen, a living room and a small toilet. On the first floor, there are two bedrooms and a bathroom. My bedroom is small but nice. I have a bed, a desk and a big window. The garden is behind the house. There are many flowers in spring.")},
        {"titulo": "My Best Friend",
         "parrafo": ("My best friend is called Daniel. He lives next door. Daniel is tall and has brown hair. We go to the same school and sit together in class. We like playing computer games and riding our bicycles in the park. He is very funny and always helps me with my maths homework.")},
        {"titulo": "My Favourite Animal",
         "parrafo": ("My favourite animal is the dog. Dogs are friendly and clever. My aunt has a dog called Alfie. He is a small brown dog with big ears. Alfie likes running in the park and playing with a ball. He eats special dog food. When I visit my aunt, Alfie always jumps and wags his tail. He is very happy.")},
        {"titulo": "In the Supermarket",
         "parrafo": ("On Saturdays, my mum and I go to the supermarket. We take a shopping trolley and walk between the aisles. We buy bread, milk, cheese, apples and bananas. My mum always checks the prices. I like the sweet aisle, but she says sweets are not good for my teeth. We pay at the till and carry the bags home.")},
        {"titulo": "My Daily Routine",
         "parrafo": ("I wake up at seven o'clock every morning. First, I have a shower and brush my teeth. Then I get dressed and eat breakfast with my family. I walk to the bus stop and go to school. In the evening, I watch television, have dinner and read a book. I go to bed at nine o'clock. I sleep eight hours every night.")},
        {"titulo": "The Weather Today",
         "parrafo": ("Today the weather is cloudy. In Britain, the weather changes very often. In the morning it rains, in the afternoon the sun shines, and in the evening it is cold. People always carry an umbrella. In winter, it sometimes snows. Children love snow because they can make a snowman. In summer, it is warm and sunny in the park.")},
        {"titulo": "At the Weekend",
         "parrafo": ("At the weekend, I do many things. On Saturday morning, I play football with my team. In the afternoon, I visit my grandparents. My grandmother makes biscuits and we drink tea together. On Sunday, my family goes for a walk in the countryside. In the evening, I prepare my school bag for Monday. I love the weekends.")},
        {"titulo": "My Bedroom",
         "parrafo": ("My bedroom is my favourite place in the house. The walls are light blue. There is a comfortable bed near the window. I have a small desk where I do my homework. My books are on a shelf above the desk. There is a lamp and a photo of my family on the wall. I keep my toys in a big box under my bed. My room is always tidy.")},
    ],

    "A2 – Elemental": [
        {"titulo": "A Weekend in the Countryside",
         "parrafo": ("Last weekend, we visited my grandmother in the countryside. The weather was lovely, so we went for a long walk across the fields. We saw sheep and cows everywhere. My grandmother cooked a traditional Sunday roast with vegetables from her garden. It was delicious. On Sunday evening, we drove back home feeling tired but very happy.")},
        {"titulo": "My Favourite Hobby",
         "parrafo": ("I really enjoy reading books about British history. I usually read in the evenings, after finishing my homework. At the moment, I am reading a novel about the Victorians. Reading helps me learn new vocabulary and improves my writing too. Last month, I joined a reading club at my school. We meet every Wednesday and talk about the books we have read.")},
        {"titulo": "A Trip to the Seaside",
         "parrafo": ("Last summer, my family and I went to Brighton for a week. We travelled by train, which took about an hour. Every day, we walked along the beach and ate fish and chips. My brother swam in the sea, but the water was freezing! We visited the famous pier and played in the amusement arcade. It was one of the best holidays of my life.")},
        {"titulo": "My First Job",
         "parrafo": ("When I was sixteen, I got my first job at a local bakery. I worked on Saturday mornings, serving customers and cleaning the shop. The work was harder than I expected, but my colleagues were kind and taught me a lot. With my first salary, I bought a new bicycle. That experience showed me how important it is to earn your own money.")},
        {"titulo": "A Rainy Afternoon",
         "parrafo": ("It was raining heavily, so we decided to stay at home. My sister suggested watching an old film, and my father made popcorn in the kitchen. We sat together on the sofa under warm blankets. Halfway through the film, the lights went out because of the storm. We ended up telling funny stories by candlelight until the electricity came back.")},
        {"titulo": "Learning to Cook",
         "parrafo": ("Last year, I decided to learn how to cook. At first, everything went wrong: I burnt the toast and undercooked the rice. However, I watched videos and practised every weekend. Now I can prepare a simple dinner for my family. Yesterday I made a shepherd's pie, and everybody said it tasted really good. Cooking is difficult, but very rewarding.")},
        {"titulo": "The Lost Puppy",
         "parrafo": ("While I was walking home from school, I found a small puppy sitting alone near the bus stop. It looked frightened and hungry, so I gave it some water and called the number on its collar. Twenty minutes later, a very grateful woman arrived. She had been looking for her puppy all afternoon. She thanked me and gave me a box of chocolates. I felt really proud.")},
        {"titulo": "My Town",
         "parrafo": ("I live in a medium-sized town in the north of England. There is a busy high street with shops, cafés and a library. The town also has a beautiful old church that was built three hundred years ago. On market days, farmers sell fresh fruit and vegetables in the square. Life here is quieter than in a big city, but I would not change it for anything.")},
        {"titulo": "A Visit to the Museum",
         "parrafo": ("Last month, our class visited the Natural History Museum in London. We saw dinosaur skeletons, precious stones and a life-sized model of a blue whale. The guide explained how fossils form over millions of years. I took many photos and bought a small dinosaur figure in the gift shop. It was a fascinating trip, and I learned more in one day than in a whole month of lessons.")},
        {"titulo": "My Pet Rabbit",
         "parrafo": ("My pet rabbit is called Clover. She has soft white fur and long ears. Every morning, I give her fresh vegetables and clean water. Rabbits need space to exercise, so we built a large hutch in the garden. Clover is very curious and follows me around the house. Taking care of an animal is a big responsibility, but it teaches you to be patient and caring.")},
    ],

    "B1 – Intermedio": [
        {"titulo": "Working in a Café",
         "parrafo": ("I have been working in a busy café in Manchester since January. Although it can be tiring, I love meeting customers from all over the world. My colleagues are friendly, and we often help each other during the busiest hours. The manager has taught me how to make the perfect cappuccino. In the future, I would like to open my own little tea shop by the seaside.")},
        {"titulo": "The Weather in Britain",
         "parrafo": ("British people are famous for talking about the weather, and there is a good reason for it: it changes constantly. One moment the sky is grey and it is drizzling, and the next the sun is shining. Because of this, most people carry an umbrella wherever they go, just in case. Forecasters try to predict the weather, but even they admit that in Britain you should always be prepared for a surprise.")},
        {"titulo": "Moving to a New City",
         "parrafo": ("Moving to a new city is exciting, but it can also be stressful. When I first arrived in Bristol, I did not know anybody, and everything seemed unfamiliar. However, I joined a local sports club and started attending language classes. Within a few months, I had made good friends and felt at home. If you ever move somewhere new, my advice is simple: say yes to every invitation.")},
        {"titulo": "The Importance of Sleep",
         "parrafo": ("Getting enough sleep is far more important than most people realise. While we sleep, our brains process the information we have learned during the day and our bodies repair themselves. Experts recommend between seven and nine hours per night for adults. Unfortunately, many people sacrifice sleep to watch television or scroll through their phones, which affects their concentration and mood the following day.")},
        {"titulo": "A Traditional Christmas",
         "parrafo": ("Christmas in Britain is full of traditions. Families decorate their houses with lights and a tree, and children hang stockings at the end of their beds. On Christmas Day, people exchange presents, pull crackers and eat roast turkey with sprouts and gravy. In the afternoon, many families watch the King's speech before falling asleep in front of the television. It is a day for eating too much and being grateful.")},
        {"titulo": "Volunteering at the Shelter",
         "parrafo": ("Every Saturday morning, I volunteer at an animal shelter near my home. When I started, I mainly cleaned the cages and fed the animals. Since then, I have learned how to look after injured birds and how to gain the trust of nervous dogs. The work is sometimes sad, but when an animal finds a new family, it makes everything worthwhile.")},
        {"titulo": "Travelling by Train",
         "parrafo": ("Travelling by train is one of the most pleasant ways to explore Britain. From the window, you can see rolling hills, historic towns and dramatic coastlines. Trains are generally punctual, although delays can happen during bad weather. Booking in advance is much cheaper than buying a ticket on the day. If you have never taken a long train journey across the countryside, you should definitely try it.")},
        {"titulo": "Learning a Musical Instrument",
         "parrafo": ("Two years ago, I decided to learn the piano. At the beginning, I could barely read the notes, and my fingers felt clumsy. I practised for thirty minutes every day, even when I did not feel like it. Now I can play several songs from memory. The secret, according to my teacher, is not talent but consistency. Progress is slow at first, but it always comes if you keep going.")},
        {"titulo": "The Local Pub",
         "parrafo": ("The pub has always been at the heart of British social life. It is not simply a place to drink; it is where friends meet, families have Sunday lunch and neighbours catch up on the local news. Many pubs organise quiz nights, which attract teams of regulars week after week. In small villages, the pub is often the only place where the whole community gathers, which explains why people fight so hard to save them.")},
        {"titulo": "An Unforgettable Match",
         "parrafo": ("Last month, I went to a football match at Wembley Stadium. The atmosphere was incredible: eighty thousand people singing, waving flags and cheering for their teams. The match was exciting from the first minute, and the winning goal came in the final seconds. When the referee blew the final whistle, half of the stadium exploded with joy while the other half sat in silence. I will never forget that night.")},
    ],

    "B2 – Intermedio alto": [
        {"titulo": "The Importance of Public Libraries",
         "parrafo": ("Public libraries have played a remarkable role in British society for centuries. Not only do they provide free access to books and the internet, but they also serve as community hubs where people of all ages can learn new skills. Despite budget cuts in recent years, many libraries have adapted by offering workshops, coding clubs and reading groups, which proves how valuable they remain to local communities throughout the country.")},
        {"titulo": "Sustainable Travel",
         "parrafo": ("More and more travellers are choosing sustainable options when planning their holidays. Rather than flying, some people now take the train across Europe, enjoying the scenery while reducing their carbon footprint. However, critics argue that slow travel is a luxury that not everyone can afford, which raises important questions about fairness in the movement towards greener tourism and who truly benefits from it.")},
        {"titulo": "The Rise of Remote Work",
         "parrafo": ("The pandemic transformed the way millions of people work. Offices that were once full of employees became empty almost overnight, and video calls replaced face-to-face meetings. Nowadays, many companies offer hybrid arrangements, allowing staff to split their time between home and the office. While some workers would never go back to commuting five days a week, others miss the social side of office life and struggle to separate work from rest at home.")},
        {"titulo": "British Humour",
         "parrafo": ("British humour can be puzzling for foreigners. It often relies on irony, understatement and self-deprecation, which means that a person complaining about the weather during a heatwave is probably joking. Where other cultures might express enthusiasm directly, the British tend to say the opposite of what they mean, expecting the listener to understand. Once you grasp this, everyday conversations become far more entertaining.")},
        {"titulo": "The Debate About Exams",
         "parrafo": ("Few topics divide teachers as much as examinations. Supporters argue that exams provide an objective measure of ability and prepare students for the pressure of adult life. Critics, on the other hand, claim that they reward memory rather than genuine understanding and disadvantage students who suffer from anxiety. Some schools have introduced coursework and projects as alternatives, yet most qualifications in Britain still depend heavily on final written papers.")},
        {"titulo": "The Power of Small Habits",
         "parrafo": ("We tend to believe that changing our lives requires dramatic decisions, yet research suggests otherwise. Tiny habits, repeated daily, gradually reshape our behaviour far more effectively than occasional bursts of motivation. Reading ten pages a day, for instance, adds up to more than a dozen books a year. The key is consistency rather than intensity: small actions that seem insignificant in isolation become extraordinary over time.")},
        {"titulo": "Cities and the Housing Crisis",
         "parrafo": ("Britain's major cities are facing a severe housing shortage. Property prices have risen far faster than wages, making it increasingly difficult for young people to buy their first home. As a result, a generation is renting well into their thirties. Governments have promised to build more affordable homes, but construction has not kept pace with demand. Many experts warn that unless radical measures are taken, inequality between generations will only deepen.")},
        {"titulo": "The Return of the Bee",
         "parrafo": ("Over the past few decades, Britain lost a significant proportion of its wild bees, largely due to intensive farming and the loss of meadows. Their decline matters enormously, since bees pollinate the crops that feed us. Recently, however, conservation projects have reported encouraging results: urban gardens, rooftop hives and wildflower corridors are helping several species recover. It is proof that even in cities, nature can bounce back if given a chance.")},
        {"titulo": "In Defence of Boredom",
         "parrafo": ("We fill every idle moment with our phones, yet boredom may deserve more respect than we give it. Psychologists have found that unstimulated minds wander into daydreams, which is precisely when creativity and problem-solving flourish. Many of history's greatest ideas emerged from long walks and quiet afternoons without entertainment. Perhaps, instead of escaping boredom at every opportunity, we should learn to tolerate it — our imagination might thank us for it.")},
        {"titulo": "The Second-Hand Revolution",
         "parrafo": ("Second-hand shopping has shed its old image and become mainstream, particularly among younger consumers. Charity shops, vintage markets and online marketplaces are thriving as people seek unique clothes at reasonable prices while reducing waste. The fashion industry is one of the world's biggest polluters, so choosing pre-owned garments is a genuinely sustainable choice. What began as a niche trend is quietly reshaping how an entire generation consumes.")},
    ],

    "C1 – Avanzado": [
        {"titulo": "The Evolution of the English Language",
         "parrafo": ("The English language has undergone profound transformations throughout its history, absorbing vocabulary from Latin, French and Norse, among countless other sources. What distinguishes British English from its American counterpart is not merely spelling or pronunciation, but subtle differences in idiom and register. Linguists contend that these variations, far from being deficiencies, enrich the language and reflect the cultural identity of their speakers.")},
        {"titulo": "The Art of the Essay",
         "parrafo": ("The essay remains one of the most demanding literary forms, requiring clarity of thought, elegance of expression and the courage to pursue an argument wherever it may lead. Its greatest practitioners, from Bacon to Orwell, understood that style is not decoration but thought itself made visible. To write well, Orwell argued, one must think clearly first; the muddled sentence is almost always the product of a muddled idea. Mastering the essay, therefore, means mastering the discipline of reasoning in public.")},
        {"titulo": "On Walking",
         "parrafo": ("Walking, that most ordinary of activities, has inspired a surprising amount of profound writing. From Wordsworth's rambles across the Lake District to modern psychogeographers drifting through city streets, writers have long sensed that thought moves at the pace of the body. There is something about putting one foot in front of the other that loosens the mind, allowing ideas to surface that no desk could ever summon. Perhaps that is why so many of our finest philosophers preferred muddy boots to cushioned armchairs.")},
        {"titulo": "The Ethics of Artificial Intelligence",
         "parrafo": ("As artificial intelligence becomes embedded in decisions ranging from medical diagnoses to recruitment, the ethical questions grow sharper. Should an algorithm be permitted to assess a person's creditworthiness, and who bears responsibility when it errs? Regulators are struggling to keep pace with technological change, and the danger is that systems trained on past data quietly perpetuate the prejudices embedded in it. What is at stake is nothing less than deciding which human values we delegate to machines.")},
        {"titulo": "Cultural Heritage and Its Discontents",
         "parrafo": ("Museums across Europe are confronting an uncomfortable inheritance. Many of their most treasured artefacts arrived through colonial channels whose legitimacy is now disputed, and campaigns for restitution are gaining momentum. Curators find themselves caught between scholarly duty and moral pressure, aware that the universal museum was founded on assumptions few still defend. How institutions navigate this reckoning may well define the relationship between culture and power in the coming decades.")},
        {"titulo": "The Attention Economy",
         "parrafo": ("In an age where attention is harvested and sold, the ability to concentrate has become a rare and valuable asset. Digital platforms are engineered by some of the world's brightest minds to capture our gaze, and they have succeeded beyond any propagandist's dreams. Critics warn that a civilisation unable to sustain attention will struggle to read deeply, think critically or deliberate together. Reclaiming our focus, they argue, is among the defining challenges of our era.")},
        {"titulo": "The Celtic Revival",
         "parrafo": ("In recent decades, languages once thought doomed have experienced an unexpected renaissance. Welsh, once banned from official life, now thrives in schools, broadcasting and public signage, while Scottish Gaelic and Cornish enjoy growing cultural prestige. This revival owes little to sentiment and much to policy: committed education, media investment and legal recognition. It demonstrates that language death is not inevitable, provided that communities and governments are willing to invest in their words.")},
        {"titulo": "On Reading Old Books",
         "parrafo": ("There is a peculiar pleasure in reading books written centuries ago, one that contemporary literature, for all its merits, cannot replicate. Old texts show us that our ancestors wrestled with the same anxieties we do — love, mortality, faith and ambition — while simultaneously revealing how radically our assumptions differ from theirs. The conversation across the centuries is, in the end, the best education in humility: it reminds us that our certainties, too, will one day look quaint.")},
        {"titulo": "The Paradox of Choice",
         "parrafo": ("Conventional wisdom holds that more choice means more freedom, yet psychologists have gathered compelling evidence to the contrary. Faced with an overwhelming array of options, people often experience paralysis, anxiety and regret, a phenomenon so common it has earned its own label. The satisfied consumer, it turns out, is frequently the one who decides quickly and stops comparing. Perhaps happiness lies not in having every possibility open, but in committing gracefully to some and letting the rest go.")},
        {"titulo": "Gardens and the British Soul",
         "parrafo": ("No other nation takes gardening quite so seriously as the British, and the reasons run deeper than hobby. The garden embodies a distinctive philosophy: patient negotiation with an unreliable climate, reverence for the modest and domestic, and a belief that beauty need not be grand to be profound. From Capability Brown's landscapes to the suburban allotment, gardening represents an ongoing conversation between order and wildness. To understand Britain, one could do worse than to observe what it grows.")},
    ],

    "C2 – Maestría": [
        {"titulo": "On the Subtlety of Irony",
         "parrafo": ("Irony, that most British of rhetorical devices, thrives on the tension between what is said and what is meant. Its deployment requires not only linguistic dexterity but also a sophisticated awareness of context and audience, for irony poorly executed is indistinguishable from sincerity. Scholars have long debated whether irony constitutes a defence mechanism against earnestness or, conversely, a vehicle through which uncomfortable truths may be uttered with plausible deniability. Either way, its mastery remains perhaps the surest hallmark of true fluency.")},
        {"titulo": "The Problem of Consciousness",
         "parrafo": ("Despite a century of neuroscience and philosophy, the so-called 'hard problem' of consciousness remains stubbornly unresolved: why should physical processes give rise to subjective experience at all? Some theorists contend that consciousness is fundamental to the universe, while sceptics dismiss the question as confused. What is undeniable is that no framework currently available — physicalist, panpsychist or dualist — has succeeded in bridging the explanatory gap between matter and felt experience, leaving the mind as the frontier it has always been.")},
        {"titulo": "The Architecture of Memory",
         "parrafo": ("Memory, far from being a faithful archive, behaves more like a skilful forger, reconstructing the past anew with every recollection. Each act of remembering subtly rewrites the trace it retrieves, incorporating subsequent knowledge, mood and suggestion. This malleability, once considered a design flaw, is increasingly understood as adaptive: a memory system immune to revision would trap us forever in the literal past. The unsettling corollary, however, is that our most cherished recollections may be, in part, elegant fictions we have authored about ourselves.")},
        {"titulo": "Elegy and the English Landscape",
         "parrafo": ("Few literary traditions are so saturated with melancholy as the English elegy, in which landscape and loss become inextricable. From Gray's churchyard to Hardy's heaths and Larkin's provincial stations, the poetry of Britain finds in its very topography a vocabulary for grief: the fading light of autumn afternoons, the half-derelict village, the train that does not stop. This is not sentimentality but a species of tough-minded consolation, an insistence that sorrow, properly observed, can be made to yield a strange and enduring beauty.")},
        {"titulo": "The Limits of Translation",
         "parrafo": ("Every translation is an act of interpretive daring, since no language maps neatly onto another; each carries within it a distinct metaphysics, a way of slicing reality that resists transport. The translator must therefore betray — the Italian aphorism traduttore, traditore is unavoidable — but betray judiciously, deciding what may be sacrificed: cadence, connotation, wit or literal sense. The finest translations succeed not by erasing their own labour but by making audible, between the lines, the original's untranslatable music.")},
        {"titulo": "On Moral Luck",
         "parrafo": ("Philosophers have long been troubled by the observation that our moral judgements are hostage to fortune. Two drivers may be equally reckless, yet only one kills a child who runs into the road; we condemn him as we do not condemn the other, whose identical negligence merely went unpunished by chance. If character is what we can control and consequence is not, then our intuitions about praise and blame appear to be metaphysically incoherent. The problem exposes a deep tension between the justice we feel and the fairness we profess to want.")},
        {"titulo": "The Sounds of Silence",
         "parrafo": ("In contemporary discourse, silence is treated largely as a deficit to be filled, yet across traditions it has been revered as the very medium of insight. Quakers worship in expectant stillness; Trappists take vows of it; composers from Cage to Takemitsu have made its structure audible. Neuroscience, for its part, confirms what contemplatives always suspected: the brain is never silent, and what we call silence is rather a rare skill of attending to the mind's own unceasing murmur.")},
        {"titulo": "Utopia and Its Shadow",
         "parrafo": ("Every utopia, critics remind us, casts the shadow of its own dystopia: the dream of a perfected society has repeatedly licensed its opposite. The twentieth century provided devastating evidence that plans for human improvement, armed with the state's full power, can flatten the very plurality they claim to serve. And yet the utopian imagination can hardly be abandoned, for a civilisation incapable of envisioning alternatives resigns itself to administering the present indefinitely. The task, perhaps, is to utopianise modestly: to imagine better worlds without erecting them on foundations of doctrine.")},
        {"titulo": "The Essay as Thought Experiment",
         "parrafo": ("At its finest, the essay is less an argument than a laboratory: a place where hypotheses about life are conducted upon the page and their consequences observed in real time. Montaigne, the form's inventor, called his essays 'attempts' precisely because he understood writing as trialling oneself before an audience. This provisionality is the genre's enduring gift — the permission it grants to think in public, to follow a thought past the point of comfort, and to arrive at conclusions one had no intention of reaching.")},
        {"titulo": "Language, Thought and the Sapir-Whorf Hypothesis",
         "parrafo": ("The proposition that language determines thought, once fashionable and then ridiculed, has returned in subtler form. Evidence that grammatical gender shifts the associations of objects, or that languages lacking absolute directions foster remarkable spatial memory, suggests that tongues do not imprison minds but do gently bend them. The contemporary consensus — that languages influence rather than dictate cognition — is admittedly less dramatic than the original hypothesis, yet it retains its subversive edge: whoever masters several grammars acquires, perhaps, several ways of being a self.")},
    ],
}
